---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout title="Security & Deployment Responsibilities"
  description="Understand ShorelineOps access controls, deployment boundaries, offline behavior, and the checks required before facility use."
  canonicalPath="/security">
  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 space-y-10 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-500">Updated September 23, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Security and deployment responsibilities</h1>
      <p class="text-lg">Review the controls, operating limits, and responsibilities that apply to your facility before using ShorelineOps with resident information.</p>
      <p>This page describes product behavior and deployment requirements. It is not a security certification, compliance determination, or service-level agreement.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">One facility per deployment</h2>
      <p>Clinical operations currently require a separate application deployment and database for each facility. Facility identifiers and account roles do not provide complete isolation within a shared clinical database. Switching facility context through a request header is disabled.</p>
      <p>Use separate database credentials, signing secrets, backups, and access policies for each installation. A shared multi-facility clinical deployment requires additional isolation work and independent validation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Accounts and protected actions</h2>
      <p>The application uses bcrypt password hashing and signed access tokens. Clinical EHR review is limited to dietitian and administrator roles. Purchasing approval and line editing use server-side permission and workflow checks.</p>
      <p>Administrators must review user access, configure available multi-factor authentication, and test session expiry on shared devices. An application role does not establish a professional credential.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Clinical decisions and recorded outcomes</h2>
      <p>Inbound changes require explicit review. Failed requests must remain unresolved until the server confirms the result. Tray verification must use a current signed ticket and available clinical data; unavailable verification requires a hold.</p>
      <p>Automated checks support facility procedures. They do not replace authorized clinical orders, physical texture checks, or professional review. Menu assessments without supporting data are shown as not evaluated.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Network outages and recovery</h2>
      <p>A local SQLite installation can operate without internet while staff remain connected to the local application server. A tablet disconnected from that server cannot verify current orders or confirm saved actions. Browser API responses are not stored in the service-worker cache.</p>
      <p>There is no guaranteed offline mutation replay. Prepare a facility-approved downtime procedure, keep required current paper records available, and reconcile recorded outcomes after reconnecting before retrying actions.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Hosting, backups, and audit records</h2>
      <p>TLS termination, database and backup encryption, retention, monitoring, restore testing, and incident response depend on the chosen hosting configuration. Validate them in the deployed environment; source code alone does not establish these safeguards.</p>
      <p>Clinical reconciliation and purchase approval record the authenticated actor with the committed decision. Audit durability and tamper resistance must be tested for the selected database and operating permissions.</p>
      <p>Availability commitments, recovery targets, support response times, and any service credits must be established in an executed service agreement. No measured uptime or recovery guarantee is represented here.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Before facility rollout</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Validate clinical review, tray holds, authorization, and recovery with approved synthetic cases.</li>
        <li>Test the actual phones, tablets, keyboards, and assistive technology used by staff.</li>
        <li>Complete a backup restore and network-outage drill.</li>
        <li>Confirm applicable privacy agreements and responsibilities with the facility and hosting provider.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Report suspected vulnerabilities through the security reporting process in the project repository. Do not include resident information or credentials in public issues.</p>
    </section>
    <nav aria-label="Related information" class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 border-t border-slate-200 pt-6">
      <a href="/pricing" class="min-h-12 inline-flex items-center text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 underline">Deployment and pricing</a>
      <a href="/privacy" class="min-h-12 inline-flex items-center text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 underline">Privacy policy</a>
      <a href="/terms" class="min-h-12 inline-flex items-center text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 underline">Terms of service</a>
    </nav>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
